is a smallest of the three and the most affluent on average, attracting the rich and famous from the local area (who tend to settle there), and from Poland, Europe and elsewhere, who come over for vacations or maintain a pied-a-terre there. Sopot has an impressive history, but remains small and committed to its original mission of leisure. Being locked between the larger Gdynia and Gdańsk and being able to take advantage of their big-city infrastructure, it retains its small-town charm and remains a very popular seaside destination in Poland.","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Sopot","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Sopot","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"short":"Sopot is a spa and beach town on the coast of Poland, built around Sopot Pier and Krzywy Domek. Gdansk next door adds Oliwa Cathedral and Westerplatte, six hours for both, with June to August the beach months.","short_src":"rewrite","facts":{"population":35049,"visit_h":4.3}},"highlights":[{"id":"hl-st-mary-s-basilica-in-gdansk","name":"St. Mary's Basilica in Gdańsk","kind":"Church","lat":54.34984,"lon":18.6535,"dist_km":11.4,"rank_score":9.63,"resolved_by":"catalogue","image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Bazylika_Mariacka_DSC01870.jpg/960px-Bazylika_Mariacka_DSC01870.jpg","author":"Gyddanyzc","licence":"CC0","licence_url":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bazylika_Mariacka_DSC01870.jpg","ok_print":true}},{"id":"hl-museum-of-world-war-ii-in-gdansk","name":"Museum of World War II in Gdańsk","kind":"Museum","lat":54.35583,"lon":18.66056,"dist_km":11.2,"rank_score":8.0,"resolved_by":"catalogue","image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Museum_of_the_Second_World_War_in_Gda%C5%84sk_2025b.jpg/960px-Museum_of_the_Second_World_War_in_Gda%C5%84sk_2025b.jpg","author":"Adrian Grycuk","licence":"CC BY 3.0 pl","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/